```python
def knapsack_01(weights, values, capacity):
    n = len(weights)
    dp = [[0] * (capacity + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]
    for i in range(1, n + 1):
        for w in range(capacity, -1, -1):
            dp[i][w] = dp[i - 1][w]
            if weights[i - 1] <= w:
                dp[i][w] = max(dp[i][w],
                               values[i - 1] + dp[i - 1][w - weights[i - 1]])
    return dp[n][capacity]
```

Note the inner loop iterates capacity in reverse — that's a deliberate choice this topic explains next, not an arbitrary style pick. The recurrence itself matches the state definition exactly: skip inherits the row above, take reaches diagonally into the row above at a smaller capacity.

Both variants' 2D tables only ever read the row directly above (or the same row, for unbounded) — never two rows back. That means the full `n+1` rows collapse to a single array of size `capacity + 1`, updated in place each iteration over items.

```python
def knapsack_01(weights, values, capacity):
    dp = [0] * (capacity + 1)
    for wt, val in zip(weights, values):
        for w in range(capacity, wt - 1, -1):
            dp[w] = max(dp[w], val + dp[w - wt])
    return dp[capacity]
```

This is an O(n·capacity)-time, O(capacity)-space solution — down from O(n·capacity) space. The loop-direction rule from the previous two slides is the only thing standing between this being correct and silently wrong.
